            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other slopes are allowed for sunny lines.\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of the set of points with a given ordinate \\(y=r\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,r)\\in T_n\\}\\,| = n+1-r\\qquad (1\\le r\\le n).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for a given abscissa \\(x=c\\) we have \\(|\\{(c,b)\\in T_n\\}| = n+1-c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Lemma 1 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\), at least two lines are **not** sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(1+n=n+1\\), hence belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe unique line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:\\; x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).  By definition a sunny line is never parallel to a line of slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) is **not** sunny.\n\nIf a single line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\), that line would have to be \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) and hence be non‑sunny.  \nAssume for contradiction that \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) is the **only** non‑sunny line in \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nAll other lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny, i.e. have slopes different from \\(-1\\).\n\nNow look at the point \\(P_3=(1,1)\\in T_n\\).  \nIf \\(P_3\\) does not lie on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) (it does not, because \\(1+1=2\\neq n+1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\)), it must be covered by a sunny line.  \nAny sunny line through \\(P_3\\) has slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nSuch a line meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) only at \\(P_3\\); consequently it cannot contain any other point of the form \\((1,b)\\) with \\(b>1\\).  \nIn particular it cannot cover the point \\((1,n)\\), which is already on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).  \n\nThus the point \\((1,2)\\) (which belongs to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+2\\le n+1\\)) must be covered either by \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) (it is not, because \\(1+2\\neq n+1\\)) or by a sunny line.  \nA sunny line through \\((1,2)\\) has slope \\(m\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\) and therefore meets the horizontal line \\(y=2\\) only at \\((1,2)\\).  \nHence it cannot also contain \\((2,2)\\), \\((3,2)\\), …, \\((n-1,2)\\).  \nRepeating this argument for each row shows that a single sunny line cannot cover more than one point of a given row, while the horizontal line \\(y=r\\) would cover all points of that row.  \n\nSince we have only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines left, they can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\) that are not already on \\(\\ell_{-1}\\).  \nBut \\(|T_n| = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}> n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\), a contradiction.  \n\nTherefore at least **two** distinct lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction for any \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers all points of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe build \\(\\mathcal L\\) as the union of three sub‑families:\n\n1. **Two fixed non‑sunny lines**  \n\n   \\[\n   L_h:\\; y=1\\qquad\\text{(horizontal, slope }0\\text{)},\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   L_v:\\; x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical, slope }\\infty\\text{)}.\n   \\]\n\n   Both are non‑sunny by definition.\n\n2. **\\(k\\) sunny lines**  \n\n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   s_1,s_2,\\dots ,s_k\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y = m_i x + b_i,\n   \\]\n\n   where we set  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i = \\frac{1}{s_i}\\quad(\\text{so }m_i\\neq 0,\\infty,-1),\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_i = 1 - m_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(S_i\\) passes through the point \\((s_i,1)\\) (because \\(1 = m_i s_i + b_i\\)) and also through \\((1,0)\\), which is **outside** \\(T_n\\).  \n   Since the slopes \\(m_i\\) are all distinct, the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct and sunny.\n\n3. **\\(n-2-k\\) additional horizontal lines**  \n\n   For each integer \\(r\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   r\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\},\n   \\]\n\n   include the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_r:\\; y=r .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines are non‑sunny and distinct from each other and from \\(L_h\\).\n\nAltogether we have  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(L_h)+1\\;(L_v)+k\\;(S_i)+(n-2-k)\\;(H_r)=n\n\\]\n\nlines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\nIt remains to verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n*Case 1: \\(b=1\\).*  \nThe point lies on the horizontal line \\(L_h\\).\n\n*Case 2: \\(a=1\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\).*  \nThe point lies on the vertical line \\(L_v\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\ge 2\\).*  \nWrite \\(b=r\\) with \\(2\\le r\\le n\\).  \n\nIf \\(r\\) is **not** among the chosen numbers \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\), then the horizontal line \\(H_r\\) belongs to our family and contains \\((a,r)\\).\n\nIf \\(r=s_i\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,r)=(a,s_i)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\) because\n\n\\[\ny = m_i x + b_i \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nr = \\frac{1}{s_i}a + \\bigl(1-\\frac{1}{s_i}\\bigr)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na = s_i\\bigl(r-1\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\((a,r)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+r\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-r\\).  \nBecause \\(r=s_i\\ge 2\\), the right‑hand side is at least \\(1\\), and the equality above shows that the integer \\(a\\) satisfying the line equation is precisely the abscissa of the point.  \nThus \\((a,r)\\) indeed belongs to \\(S_i\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, so the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Lemma 3 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\))\n\n*Statement.*  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) can contain \\(n-1\\) or \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, to the contrary, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal L\\) has at most one non‑sunny line.  \nBy Lemma 1 at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, so such a family cannot exist.  \nConsequently the numbers of sunny lines possible are at most \\(n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 shows that any admissible configuration must have at most \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 2 provides, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit construction achieving exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 rules out the remaining possibilities \\(k=n-1,n\\).\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
