oof.\n\n4. **Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).**  \n   *Lemma 3* combines Lemma 1 with a counting argument: if only one line were non‑sunny, then the two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) would have to share that line, which would force its slope to be \\(-1\\), contradicting the definition of a sunny line. Hence at least two non‑sunny lines are necessary, ruling out \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nThe three lemmas together give the exact set of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis), from \\(\\infty\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and from \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nAll other slopes are allowed for sunny lines.\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of the set of points with a given ordinate \\(y=r\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{(a,r)\\in T_n\\}\\,| = n+1-r\\qquad (1\\le r\\le n).\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for a given abscissa \\(x=c\\) we have \\(|\\{(c,b)\\in T_n\\}| = n+1-c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### Lemma 1 (At least two non‑sunny lines are necessary)\n\n*Statement.*  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\), at least two lines are **not** sunny; consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n),\\qquad P_2=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth satisfy \\(1+n=n+1\\), hence belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe unique line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{-1}:\\; x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).  By definition a sunny line is never parallel to a line of slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) is **not** sunny.\n\nIf a single line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained both \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\), that line would have to be \\(\\ell_{-1}\\) and hence be non‑sunny.  \nAssume f