 Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nRecall that a line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(T_n\\) (they satisfy \\(1+1\\le n+1\\), etc.).\n\n*The line through any two of these points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\):*  \n\n\\[\n\\overline{P_1P_2}:\\;x=1\\;( \\text{slope } \\infty),\\qquad\n\\overline{P_1P_3}:\\;y=1\\;( \\text{slope }0),\\qquad\n\\overline{P_2P_3}:\\;x+y=3\\;( \\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\nSince the three points are distinct, covering them requires at least three distinct lines, and at most one of those three lines can be sunny. Consequently **at least two of the required \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny**.\n\nTherefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and prove that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Two fixed non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\; y=1 ,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (slopes \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\)).  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(1\\) lies on \\(\\ell_2\\); every point whose second coordinate equals \\(1\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points not yet covered\n\nA point \\((a,b