l{T}\\)).\n\nHence the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2 + k + (n-2-k)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly the \\(n-2-k\\) lines \\(S_t\\) are sunny, i.e. the number of sunny lines equals \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.5  Verification of coverage\n\n*Points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\):* lie on \\(\\ell_1\\) or \\(\\ell_2\\).  \n*Points \\((1+r,1+s)\\) with \\(r+s=t\\):*  \n – if \\(t\\in\\mathcal{T}\\) they lie on the retained line \\(L_t\\);  \n – if \\(t\\notin\\mathcal{T}\\) they lie on the sunny line \\(S_t\\) that was defined precisely to pass through that point.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nWe have shown:\n\n* At least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, so \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) lines exists with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
