\{S_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) we can replace a sunny line by a non\u2011sunny one without losing the covering property:  \n\n   * replace a sunny line that contains \\(Q_i\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) (if \\(i\\ge2\\)) or by the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\) (if \\(n+1-i\\ge2\\)).  \n   * the new line is distinct from the already present \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\), and it still covers the point \\(Q_i\\).  \n\n   Each such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while preserving distinctness and the covering condition. Repeating the operation we obtain configurations with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines, for all \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   Hence every integer in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) is attainable.\n\nCombining Lemma\u202f1 (the upper bound) with Lemma\u202f2 (the construction) yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb N,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) consists of the integer lattice points inside (and on) the right\u2011angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=1+2+\\dots +n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nd_h:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad d_v:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad d_d:\\;x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\]\n\nAll other lines are **sunny**.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 an upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nn_h=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nn_v=\\#\\{\\text{vertical lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nn_d=\\#\\{\\text{lines parallel to }x+y=0\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nn_h+n_v+n_d=n-k . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 1.** A horizontal (resp. vertical) line contains at most one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nIndeed, if \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b)\\) with \\(a\\neq a'\\) were both in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), then  \n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a'+b\\le n+1\\) would give \\(a+a'\\le 2(n+1-b)\\).  \nSince \\(a,a'\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\), the only possibility is \\(a=a'\\), a contradiction.  \nThe same argument works for vertical lines.\n\nHence the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can be covered by the horizontal and vertical lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nn_h+n_v . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n**Claim 2.** A line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nSuch a line has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with an integer constant \\(c\\).  \nIf it contains two distinct points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), then \\(a+b=a'+b'=c\\).  \nBecause \\(a,b,a',b'\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), the only way to have two different points is to have the symmetric pair  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad (a',b')=(n+1-i,i)\n\\]\n\nfor some \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\).  Thus at most two points lie on such a line.\n\nConsequently the diagonal\u2011parallel lines cover at most \\(2n_d\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nCombining the three claims we obtain the estimate  \n\n\\[\n\\text{points covered by non\u2011sunny lines}\\le n_h+n_v+2n_d . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nA sunny line is **not** parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, therefore it cannot be horizontal, vertical, nor have slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most one point of each of the three families of lines described above, i.e. it can contain at most three points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n(Indeed, if a sunny line contained four points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), two of them would have the same \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate or the same \\(y\\)\u2011coordinate or would satisfy \\(x+y\\) equal, forcing the line to be parallel to one of the forbidden directions.)\n\nHence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(3k\\) points, and from (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le (n_h+n_v+2n_d)+3k . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) to eliminate \\(n_h+n_v\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le (n-k-n_d)+2n_d+3k = n+2k+n_d . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n_d\\ge 0\\), inequality (6) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 \\le n+2k+n_d \\le n+2k+(n-k) = 2n+k .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nk \\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n*Construction for \\(k=n-2\\).*  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) lies on one of these two lines.  \nThe points that are not yet covered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nQ_i=(i,n+1-i),\\qquad i=2,3,\\dots ,n-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(n-2\\) of them.  \nFor each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;\\text{the line through }Q_i\\text{ and }Q_{i+1}.\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n+1-i)-(n-i)}{(i+1)-i}= -1+\\frac{1}{i},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nFor the last point \\(Q_{n-1}\\) choose any line through it that is not parallel to the three forbidden directions (for instance the line through \\(Q_{n-1}\\) and \\((2,2)\\)).  \nDenote this line by \\(S_{n-1}\\); it is also sunny.\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-2}= \\{L_1,L_2\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.  \n\n*Reducing the number of sunny lines.*  \n\nAssume we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) with \\(t\\) sunny lines (\\(t\\ge 1\\)).  \nPick a sunny line \\(S\\) that contains a point \\(Q_i\\) from (7).  \nReplace \\(S\\) by the vertical line \\(x=i\\) if \\(i\\ge2\\); otherwise replace it by the horizontal line \\(y=n+1-i\\) (the two possibilities are both admissible because at least one of the numbers \\(i,n+1-i\\) is \\(\\ge2\\)).  \n\nThe new line is distinct from the already existing \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\), so the family still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \nIt continues to cover the point \\(Q_i\\) (now by the new vertical/horizontal line) and all other points remain covered because each of the remaining sunny lines contains at least two points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nThus one sunny line can be turned into a non\u2011sunny line without destroying the covering property.  \nRepeating this operation we can decrease the number of sunny lines from \\(n-2\\) down to any prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n\nHence for every such \\(k\\) a suitable configuration exists. \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Final answer  \n\nLemma\u202f1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma\u202f2 exhibits, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration attaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
