ne \\(y=-x+4\\) (which contains only \\((2,2)\\)) by the sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   All points are still covered, and exactly one line is sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=1\\).\n\n6. **Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   The argument of step\u202f3 shows that any admissible family must contain the two axis lines and the \\(n-3\\) distinct \\(-1\\)\u2011slope lines; that already uses \\(n-1\\) lines, leaving at most one line that could be sunny. Hence \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible.\n\nCombining the necessary bound \\(k\\le 1\\) with the explicit constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Counting the points that must be covered  \n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\sum_{t=1}^{n}t=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A first bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1)\\in T_n\n\\]\n\nlie on the three non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \nA sunny line can contain at most one of them, because any two of the three are already collinear on a non\u2011sunny line.  \nThus at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, giving  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Diagonal argument \u2013 why many \\(-1\\)\u2011slope lines are unavoidable  \n\nFor an integer \\(s\\ge 4\\) set  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s,\\ a,b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(D_s\\) lie on the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; y=-x+s,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Lemma 1.*  \nIf a line \\(\\ell\\) does **not** have slope \\(-1\\), then \\(|\\ell\\cap D_s|\\le 1\\) for every \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points of the same diagonal \\(D_s\\) have coordinates \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a+b=a'+b'=s\\).  \nThe slope of the line through them is \\((b'-b)/(a'-a)=-(a'-a)/(a'-a)=-1\\).  \nHence a line of slope different from \\(-1\\) cannot contain two distinct points of the same diagonal. \u220e\n\nFor \\(s\\ge 5\\) we have \\(|D_s|\\ge 2\\) (e.g. \\((2,s-2)\\) and \\((3,s-3)\\)).  \nConsequently, to cover **all** points of such a diagonal we must use at least one line of slope \\(-1\\); a line of another slope could cover at most one of its points, leaving at least one uncovered.\n\nThere are exactly  \n\n\\[\n(n+1)-5+1 = n-3\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nintegers \\(s\\) with \\(5\\le s\\le n+1\\); therefore we need at least \\(n-3\\) distinct lines of slope \\(-1\\) to cover the diagonals \\(D_5,D_6,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\).\n\nIn addition, the points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) (i.e. the sets \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) and \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\)) cannot be covered by any line of slope \\(-1\\); at least one vertical line \\(x=1\\) and one horizontal line \\(y=1\\) are required.  \n\nThus any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-3) \\text{ lines of slope } -1 \\;+\\; 1 \\text{ vertical line } + 1 \\text{ horizontal line } = n-1\n\\]\n\nnon\u2011sunny lines. Hence the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTogether with (2) we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* \\(\\ell_{x}: x=1\\) (vertical, slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* \\(\\ell_{y}: y=1\\) (horizontal, slope \\(0\\));\n* For each integer \\(s\\) with \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{s}: y=-x+s .\n  \\]\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{x}\\);\n* else if \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{y}\\);\n* else \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Put \\(s=a+b\\); then \\(4\\le s\\le n+1\\) and \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_{s}\\).\n\nThus every required point lies on at least one of the lines, and all lines are non\u2011sunny. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the two axis lines \\(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y}\\) and the diagonal lines \\(\\ell_{s}\\) for \\(5\\le s\\le n+1\\) (these are \\(n-3\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nReplace the diagonal \\(\\ell_{4}\\) (which contains only the point \\((2,2)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\sigma\\) has slope \\(1\\) (neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), passes through \\((2,2)\\), and is distinct from all other lines.\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n2\\;(\\ell_{x},\\ell_{y})\\;+\\;(n-3)\\;(\\ell_{s},\\;5\\le s\\le n+1)\\;+\\;1\\;(\\sigma)=n .\n\\]\n\nExactly one line (\\(\\sigma\\)) is sunny, so \\(k=1\\).\n\n*Verification of coverage.*  \nAll points with a coordinate equal to \\(1\\) are covered as before.  \nFor a point with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and sum \\(s\\ge 5\\) we use the line \\(\\ell_{s}\\).  \nThe remaining point \\((2,2)\\) (the only element of \\(D_4\\)) lies on \\(\\sigma\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the conditions contained at least two sunny lines.  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 and the counting