herefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-3-d).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   On the other hand the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   n = d\\;+\\;(n-3-d)\\;+\\;(\\text{lines of other slopes}),\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. we have exactly \\(n-3\\) diagonals (with \\(s\\ge5\\)) plus possibly the two short diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\) (which are not forced).  \n   Since the family contains only \\(n\\) lines, the number of *other* lines (those not among the \\(n-3\\) long diagonals) is at most \\(3\\).  \n   In particular  \n\n   \\[\n   n-3-d\\le 1,\n   \\]\n\n   because each missing long diagonal consumes at least two sunny lines, and we have at most three non\u2011diagonal lines altogether.  \n   Hence \\(n-3-d\\le 1\\) and from (1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le 1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (2) shows that **no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines**.\n\n5. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_2,\\;\\ell_3,\\;\\ell_4,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1},\n   \\qquad\\text{where }\\ell_s:\\;y=-x+s .\n   \\]\n\n   Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for some \\(s\\) in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  \n   All lines have slope \\(-1\\); therefore \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Existence for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Keep the two short diagonals \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\) and the long diagonals \\(\\ell_5,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) (that is, all diagonals except \\(\\ell_4\\)).  \n   Replace \\(\\ell_4\\) (which contains only the point \\((2,2)\\)) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma:\\; y = x .\n   \\]\n\n   The family now consists of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{\\ell_2,\\;\\ell_3}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\ell_5,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}}_{\\text{non\u2011sunny, }n-3\\text{ lines}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\sigma}_{\\text{sunny}},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, with precisely one sunny line. 