ntains \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nd:=|\\{\\,\\ell_s\\in\\mathcal F\\mid s\\ge 5\\,\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of the long diagonals that actually belong to \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nEach of the remaining \\(n-3-d\\) long diagonals is **not** present in \\(\\mathcal F\\); by Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line can contribute at most one point of such a diagonal, so covering all its points requires at least two sunny lines. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;2\\,(n-3-d). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of  \n\n* the \\(d\\) long diagonals that are present,\n* the two short diagonals \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) (they may or may not be present, but they are not needed for the bound),\n* the remaining \\(n-(d+2)\\) lines, which are either sunny or non\u2011sunny but are not among the long diagonals.\n\nThus the total number of lines that are **not** long diagonals is at most  \n\n\\[\nn-(d) = (n-3-d)+3 .\n\\]\n\nSince each missing long diagonal forces at least two sunny lines, (4) together with the fact that we have at most three non\u2011long lines yields\n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-3-d)\\le 3 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; n-3-d\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nPlugging this inequality into (4) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence **no admissible configuration can contain two or more sunny lines**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the bound  \n\n#### (i)  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them have slope \\(-1\\), so they are non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_s\\).  \nThus every required point lies on at least one of the chosen lines, and \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n#### (ii)  \\(k=1\\)\n\nKeep the two short diagonals \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\) and the long diagonals \\(\\ell_5,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) (that is, all diagonals except \\(\\ell_4\\)).  \nReplace the missing diagonal \\(\\ell_4\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe resulting family consists of  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_2,\\;\\ell_3,\\;\\ell_5,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1},\\;\\sigma,\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, with precisely one sunny line (\\(\\sigma\\)).  \nCoverage:\n\n* Points with sum \\(s\\ge5\\) lie on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n* The point \\((2,2)\\) (the only element of \\(D_4\\)) lies on \\(\\sigma\\).\n* Points with sum \\(2\\) or \\(3\\) lie on \\(\\ell_2\\) or \\(\\ell_3\\).\n\nHence all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer  \n\nFrom (5) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le 1\\); the explicit constructions above show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) can indeed be achieved.  \nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
