------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line \\(L\\) does **not** have slope \\(-1\\), then \\(|L\\cap D_s|\\le 1\\) for every \\(s\\).\n\n*Proof.* Two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in D_s\\) satisfy \\(a+b=a'+b'=s\\). The slope of the line through them is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b'-b}{a'-a}= \\frac{-(a'-a)}{a'-a}=-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence a line whose slope differs from \\(-1\\) cannot contain two points of the same diagonal. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal F\\) be a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nDenote by  \n\n* \\(k\\) the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) (i.e. lines whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\));\n* \\(m\\) the number of diagonals \\(\\ell_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) that are **not** members of \\(\\mathcal F\\).\n\nThere are \\(n-1\\) diagonals in total.  \nIf a diagonal \\(\\ell_s\\) is absent, all its points must be covered by other lines. By Lemma 1 each sunny line can contribute at most one point of that diagonal, and a non‑sunny line that is not \\(\\ell_s\\) can contribute at most one point as well (otherwise it would have slope \\(-1\\)). Since \\(|D_s|\\ge 3\\) for every \\(s\\ge 4\\), covering an absent diagonal requires at least three sunny lines. Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\;\\ge\\;3m. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal F\\) consists of\n\n* the \\(n-1-m\\) present diagonals,\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n* possibly some additional non‑sunny lines that are not among the diagonals.\n\nBecause the total number of distinct lines is \\(n\\) and we already have \\(n-1\\) different diagonals, there can be **at most one** extra non‑sunny line. Hence\n\n\\[\nn \\ge (n-1-m)+k \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k \\le m+1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) and (2) we obtain \\(3m\\le m+1\\), i.e. \\(m\\le 0\\). Therefore \\(m=0\\); no diagonal may be omitted. Substituting \\(m=0\\) into (2) 