\nS_{n-1}'=\\{(a',b)\\mid a',b\\ge 1,\\;a'+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly \\(S_{n-1}\\) after the translation \\((x,y)\\mapsto (x+1,y)\\).\n\nApply the induction hypothesis to \\(S_{n-1}\\): we obtain a family\n\\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}\\) of \\(n-1\\) lines, one non‑sunny, covering \\(S_{n-1}\\).\nTranslate every line of \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\); the\nresulting family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines,\nstill exactly one of which is non‑sunny (the vertical line \\(x=2\\)), and it\ncovers \\(S_{n-1}'\\).\n\nFinally the union  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\\{\\;x=1\\;\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is non‑sunny, and it covers\n\\(S_n\\) (the first column by \\(x=1\\) and the remaining points by\n\\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{+}\\)).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) supplied by Lemma 1; it has exactly\n\\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  Choose any \\(k\\) of those sunny lines and replace each\nby a *dummy* sunny line that does not meet any point of \\(S_n\\).  For a fixed\nadmissible slope \\(\\alpha\\) the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{c\\in\\mathbb R\\mid \\exists (a,b)\\in S_n\\;,\\;b=\\alpha a+c\\}\n\\]\n\nis finite; picking any other real number gives a line \\(y=\\alpha x+c\\) disjoint\nfrom \\(S_n\\).  The dummy lines are distinct because we choose distinct slopes\nfor them.  After the replacement we still have \\(n\\) distinct lines; the\nnon‑sunny line is unchanged, and precisely \\(k\\) of the remaining lines are\nsunny (the dummy lines are also sunny).  Since the dummy lines avoid \\(S_n\\),\nthe points that were covered before the replacement remain covered by the\nunchanged lines.  Hence the new family covers all of \\(S_n\\) and contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover \\(S_n\\); consequently\n\\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal G\\) of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\nDefine the three subsets of \\(S_n\\) as in (3):\n\n\\[\nA=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nB=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nC=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(A\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), all points of\n\\(B\\) on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and all points of \\(C\\) on the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Because a sunny line is not parallel to any of\nthese three directions, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each\nset \\(A,B,C\\).  Hence the total number of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that can be\ncovered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|A|+|B|+|C|-1 = n+n+(n-1)-1 = 3n-1,\n\\]\n\nthe subtraction of \\(1\\) accounting for the unique common point \\((1,1)\\) of\n\\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nOn the other hand  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-1\n\\]\n\n(equivalently \\(n^{2}-5n+2>0\\); this holds for \\(n=3,4,5\\) by direct\ncomputation and for all larger \\(n\\) because the quadratic is positive).  Thus\n\\(|S_n|\\) exceeds the maximal number of points that \\(n\\) sunny lines can\ncover, a contradiction.  Therefore a covering by only sunny lines does not\nexist, i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 2 provides, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\), a configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct