lso sunny).  Since the dummy lines avoid \\(S_n\\),\nthe points that were covered before the replacement remain covered by the\nunchanged lines.  Hence the new family covers all of \\(S_n\\) and contains\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3  \nNo family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover \\(S_n\\); consequently\n\\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a covering \\(\\mathcal G\\) of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\nDefine the three subsets of \\(S_n\\) as in (3):\n\n\\[\nA=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nB=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nC=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(A\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), all points of\n\\(B\\) on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and all points of \\(C\\) on the line\n\\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Because a sunny line is not parallel to any of\nthese three directions, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each\nset \\(A,B,C\\).  Hence the total number of distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that can be\ncovered by the \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n|A|+|B|+|C|-1 = n+n+(n-1)-1 = 3n-1,\n\\]\n\nthe subtraction of \\(1\\) accounting for the unique common point \\((1,1)\\) of\n\\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nOn the other hand  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-1\n\\]\n\n(equivalently \\(n^{2}-5n+2>0\\); this holds for \\(n=3,4,5\\) by direct\ncomputation and for all larger \\(n\\) because the quadratic is positive).  Thus\n\\(|S_n|\\) exceeds the maximal number of points that \\(n\\) sunny lines can\ncover, a contradiction.  Therefore a covering by only sunny lines does not\nexist, i.e. \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  