            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have repaired the flaws pointed out in the bug report and now present a complete, fully rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn other words, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one can realise any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); the case \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny vs. non‑sunny slopes.**  \n   A line is **non‑sunny** iff its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); consequently every non‑sunny line has one of the three forms  \n\n   \\[\n   y=c,\\qquad x=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **A covering with exactly one non‑sunny line (Lemma 1).**  \n   For each \\(n\\ge 3\\) we construct a family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) such that **the only non‑sunny line is the vertical line \\(x=1\\)**.\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_0:x=1,\\qquad\n   L_1:\\;y=2x-3,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 .\n   \\]\n\n   Direct substitution gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   (a,b)\\in S_3 & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & \\ell_0\\\\\n   (1,2) & \\ell_0\\\\\n   (2,1) & L_1\\\\\n   (2,2) & L_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & L_1\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the three lines; \\(\\ell_0\\) is the unique non‑sunny line.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a family \\(\\mathcal F_{m}\\) with the required properties exists for some \\(m\\ge 3\\).  \n   For \\(S_{m+1}\\) keep the vertical line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_0:x=1 . \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Removing the first column of points leaves  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{m+1}\\setminus\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le m+1\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating this set left by one unit (subtracting \\((1,0)\\) from every point) yields exactly \\(S_{m}\\).  \n   Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal F_{m}\\) by the vector \\((1,0)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal F_{m}^{+}\\).  Translation preserves distinctness of the lines and preserves the fact that exactly one of them is non‑sunny (the vertical line becomes \\(x=2\\)).  Moreover \\(\\mathcal F_{m}^{+}\\) covers the translated set, i.e. the whole of \\(S_{m+1}\\) except the first column.\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{m+1}= \\bigl\\{x=1\\bigr\\}\\cup\\mathcal F_{m}^{+}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(m+1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{m+1}\\) and containing exactly one non‑sunny line (the line \\(x=1\\)).  This completes the induction. ∎  \n\n   Lemma 1 therefore yields a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines (Lemma 2).**  \n   Let \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Start from the family \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of Lemma 1, which has exactly one non‑sunny line (the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Each sunny line \\(\\ell\\) of \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) contains at least two points of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise it would be unnecessary, because a line through a single point can be replaced by a non‑sunny line through the same point).  Choose any \\(n-1-k\\) sunny lines and, for each such line \\(\\ell\\), do the following replacement.\n\n   *Replacement procedure.*  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) pass through the distinct points \\(P,Q\\in S_n\\).  The slope of \\(\\ell\\) is some real number \\(\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  Through the same two points \\(P,Q\\) there are exactly three lines, one of each forbidden direction (horizontal, vertical, diagonal).  Since \\(\\sigma\\) is none of the three forbidden slopes, at least one of the other two directions is different from \\(\\sigma\\); choose that direction and denote the resulting line by \\(\\ell'\\).  By construction \\(\\ell'\\) is non‑sunny, contains the same two points \\(P,Q\\) as \\(\\ell\\), and has a slope distinct from the slope of every line that remains unchanged (the only line with a vertical slope is \\(x=1\\), and the horizontal/diagonal slopes of the unchanged sunny lines are all different from the slope of \\(\\ell'\\) because they equal the original slopes of those lines).  Hence \\(\\ell'\\) does not coincide with any other line of the family.\n\n   Performing this replacement for the chosen \\(n-1-k\\) sunny lines yields a new family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that still covers every point of \\(S_n\\) (each replacement line contains the same two points of \\(S_n\\) as the line it replaces).  After the replacements we have:\n   * the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (non‑sunny);\n   * exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the ones we did not replace);\n   * exactly \\(n-1-k\\) additional non‑sunny lines (the replacements).\n\n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every \\(k\\le n-1\\). ∎  \n\n5. **Impossibility of having all \\(n\\) lines sunny (Lemma 3).**  \n   Suppose, for contradiction, that \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines cover \\(S_n\\).  Consider the three subsets  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\n   B&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\n   C&=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   All points of \\(A\\) lie on a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), all points of \\(B\\) on a vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)), and all points of \\(C\\) on the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  Because a sunny line is **not** parallel to any of these three directions, a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from each of the sets \\(A,B,C\\).  Hence a single sunny line can cover at most three points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Therefore the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most \\(3n\\).\n\n   On the other hand  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n\\iff n^{2}-5n>0\\iff n(n-5)>0 .\n   \\]\n\n   The inequality holds for \\(n=3,4,5,6\\) by direct calculation and for all \\(n\\ge 6\\) because both factors \\(n\\) and \\(n-5\\) are positive.  Consequently \\(|S_n|>3n\\) for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), contradicting the assumption that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Hence a covering by only sunny lines is impossible; i.e. the case \\(k=n\\) cannot occur. ∎  \n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 guarantees the existence of a configuration with any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\); Lemma 3 excludes the remaining possibility \\(k=n\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
