), and a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) only the point with that particular \\(b\\).\n\nHence to cover the \\((n-2)\\) points we need at least \\((n-2)\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \nSince we assumed that at most one line is non‑sunny, we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\n1\\ge n-2,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nTherefore at least two of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \nConsequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) such that \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The two non‑sunny lines\n\nTake the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_V:\\;x=1\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_H:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (slopes \\(\\infty\\) and \\(0\\), respectively).\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with  \n\n\\[\n2\\le i\\le k+1,\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{i-1}\\,x + \\frac{n+2}{i-1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,\\,n+2-i)\\) because substituting \\(x=i\\) into (2) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{1}{i-1}\\,i + \\frac{n+2}{i-1}\n   = \\frac{-i + n+2}{i-1}\n   = n+2-i .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(-\\frac{1}{i-1}\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) (the latter would require \\(i-1=1\\), i.e. \\(i=2\\); but for \\(i=2\\) the slope is \\(-1\\), so we **exclude** \\(i=2\\) from the sunny family).  \nHence we start the index at \\(i=3\\).  \nTo accommodate the case \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we treat them separately below.\n\nThus, for \\(k\\ge 2\\) we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_3,\\dots ,L_{k+2}\\).  \nEach has a different slope because the denominator \\(i-1\\) is different, and they are distinct because they pass through distinct points of the line \\(x+y=n+2\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Completing the set to \\(n\\) lines\n\nWe already have two non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k+2\\) lines.  \nSince \\(k\\le n-2\\), we need to add \\(n-(k+2)\\) further lines.  \nThese extra lines can be taken arbitrarily from the non‑sunny families, provided they are distinct from the ones already used.  \nA convenient choice is the family of lines with slope \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\nM_j:\\; x+y = j,\\qquad j=2,3,\\dots ,n-(k+2)+1 .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny, distinct, and none coincides with \\(L_V\\) or \\(L_H\\).\n\nThus the final collection consists of  \n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\);\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) defined in (2) (for \\(k\\ge 2\\); when \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) we simply omit them);\n* the \\(n-(k+2)\\) lines \\(x+y=j\\) listed above.\n\nAltogether we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n#### 2.4  Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(L_V\\) or \\(L_H\\) respectively.\n\n*If \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\)*, then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nHence \\(a+b\\) is an integer between \\(4\\) and \\(n\\).  \nIf \\(a+b\\) equals one of the constants used for the lines \\(x+y=j\\) (the non‑sunny diagonal lines), the point lies on that line.\n\nOtherwise \\(a+b=n+2\\).  \nIn this case the point belongs to the line \\(L_i\\) where \\(i=a\\) (note that \\(a\\ge 2\\) and \\(a\\le n\\)).  \nIf \\(i\\le k+2\\) the point is on the sunny line \\(L_i\\); if \\(i>k+2\\) then \\(a+b=n+2\\) exceeds the largest diagonal constant we introduced, but such a point cannot occur because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is indeed covered.\n\nConsequently, for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a configuration satisfying the required conditions exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFrom the upper bound (1) we have \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction of Section 2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.  \nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
