\(m\\) distinct lines from the union \\(\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D\\); denote this set by \\(\\mathcal N\\).”\n\n**Issue – Justification Gap:**  \nThe phrase “first \\(m\\) distinct lines” is ambiguous because no ordering of the union is specified. The later covering argument (Case 1 and Case 2) relies on the presence of **all** horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/3\\rfloor\\) and **all** vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,\\lfloor m/3\\rfloor\\) inside \\(\\mathcal N\\). If the arbitrary “first \\(m\\)” happen to consist mainly of diagonal lines, some of those horizontal/vertical lines could be omitted, breaking the claim that points with small \\(a\\) or \\(b\\) are covered. A rigorous construction must explicitly state that \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains **all** lines of \\(\\mathcal H\\) and \\(\\mathcal V\\) (or at least enough of them) together with enough diagonal lines to reach total size \\(m\\). This is not proved.\n\n*We therefore record a Justification Gap here.*\n\n---\n\n### 7. Claim about uncovered points\n\n> “*Claim.* Every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on a line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) except possibly the points  \n> \\(P_{r}=(r,\\;m+2-r),\\;r=1,2,\\dots ,k\\).”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error:**  \nThe proof of the claim proceeds by case analysis. Cases 1 and 2 are fine **provided** the horizontal and vertical lines mentioned are indeed in \\(\\mathcal N\\) (see previous gap). The crucial part is **Case 3b**:\n\n> “Because \\(a,b>\\lfloor m/3\\rfloor\\), the only way for \\(a+b\\) to be at most \\(m+k+1\\) is that \\(a+b=m+2,\\dots ,m+k+1\\).  \n> Writing \\(a+b=m+2+r\\) with \\(0\\le r\\le k-1\\) and using \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we obtain the **unique** solution \\((a,b)=(r+1,m+1-r)\\).”\n\nThe assertion of uniqueness is false. Example: let \\(m=5\\) (so \\(\\lfloor m/3\\rfloor=1\\)) and \\(k=2\\). Then \\(a,b>1\\) means \\(a,b\\ge2\\). Take sum \\(a+b= m+2 =7\\). The pairs \\((2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2)\\) all satisfy the conditions, not just \\((2,5)\\