his line from being selected as a sunny line; it would actually be a non‑sunny diagonal line, contradicting the claim that exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines are added. The argument that “the slope \\(-1\\) never occurs” is therefore insufficient.\n\n* **Slope \\(\\infty\\):** Since the line is written in slope‑intercept form, vertical lines are indeed excluded.\n\nBecause the exclusion of slope \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) is not rigorously established, there is a **Justification Gap** here.\n\n#### 8.2. Coverage of the points \\(P_{r}\\)\n\nThe algebraic substitution shows that each \\(S_{r}\\) passes through the point \\(P_{r}\\). This part is correct **provided** the lines are indeed distinct and sunny.\n\n#### 8.3. Distinctness of the sunny lines\n\nThe claim that the fractions \\(\\frac{r-1}{k-r+1}\\) are pairwise different is plausible but not proved. A short calculation shows that equality would imply \\((r_{1}-1)(k-r_{2}+1)=(r_{2}-1)(k-r_{1}+1)\\), which reduces to \\((r_{1}-r_{2})(k+1)=0\\), forcing \\(r_{1}=r_{2\\!}\\). This argument is missing, so we note a **Justification Gap**.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Final family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) and conclusion\n\n> “\\(\\mathcal L_{k}:=\\mathcal N\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{k}\\}\\). … Thus a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).”\n\nBecause the **Critical Error** in Step 7 shows that \\(\\mathcal N\\) does **not** cover all points of \\(T_{n}\\) except the \\(P_{r}\\), the addition of the sunny lines does **not** guarantee full coverage. Consequently the conclusion is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Errors\n\n1. **Critical Error** – The claim that the only uncovered points after placing the \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines are the \\(k\\) points \\(P_{r}\\) is false; many other points remain uncovered, invalidating the construction.\n\n2. **Justification Gap** – The selection rule “first \\(m\\) distinct lines” does not guarantee inclusion of the necessary horizontal and