"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the flaw pointed out in the bug report (the line \\(S_i : y = x-i+1\\) does **not** contain the whole vertical segment \\(\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}\\)).  \nThe original construction is therefore incorrect.  \nBelow I give a **new, fully rigorous construction** that works for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nand proves that exactly these values of \\(k\\) are attainable.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Two families of lines that together cover all required points.**  \n   *Family\u202fA* (non\u2011sunny): the \\(n\\) lines  \n   \\[\n   A_c:\\; y=-x+c ,\\qquad c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(A_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  \n   For any lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(b=-a+(a+b)\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the unique line \\(A_{a+b}\\).  \n   Consequently the family (1) covers **all** points of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **A sunny line that can replace a non\u2011sunny line without losing any point.**  \n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_c:\\; y = x + (c-2) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(S_c\\) has slope \\(+1\\) and is therefore sunny.  \n   It passes through the two lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_c=(1,c-1),\\qquad Q_c=(c-1,1) .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Observe that both \\(P_c\\) and \\(Q_c\\) also belong to the non\u2011sunny line \\(A_c\\) (since \\(c-1= -1 + c\\)).  \n   Moreover, every point of \\(T_n\\) that lies on \\(A_c\\) is of the form \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\).  \n   Such a point is either \\(P_c\\) or \\(Q_c\\) or lies on the **intersection** of the two sunny lines \\(S_{a+1}\\) and \\(S_{b+1}\\):\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=\\bigl(a,\\;a+(a+1-2)\\bigr)=\\bigl(b+(b+1-2),\\;b\\bigr).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   In other words, for every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) there exist sunny lines \\(S_{a+1}\\) and \\(S_{b+1}\\) that both contain \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   Hence **if we delete the non\u2011sunny line \\(A_c\\) and keep all sunny lines \\(S_{i}\\) for \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), every point of \\(T_n\\) that was on \\(A_c\\) is still covered** (by at least one of the two sunny lines appearing in (5)).  \n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines.**  \n\n   * Choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   * Keep the sunny lines \\(\\{S_c\\mid c\\in I\\}\\).  \n   * For every \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus I\\) keep the original non\u2011sunny line \\(A_c\\).  \n\n   The resulting family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L(I)=\\bigl\\{S_c\\mid c\\in I\\bigr\\}\\cup\n                \\bigl\\{A_c\\mid c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus I\\bigr\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (the sets \\(\\{S_c\\}\\) and \\(\\{A_c\\}\\) are disjoint because they have different slopes).  \n   By the discussion in step\u202f2, deleting any collection of non\u2011sunny lines \\(A_c\\) (i.e. replacing them by the corresponding sunny lines \\(S_c\\)) does **not** destroy the covering of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Therefore every family \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   The number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L(I)\\) is precisely \\(|I|=k\\).\n\n   Since any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised as the cardinality of a suitable subset \\(I\\), we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for **all** such \\(k\\).\n\n4. **The extreme case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   Take \\(I=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) (i.e. replace every non\u2011sunny line by its sunny counterpart).  \n   Then \\(\\mathcal L(I)=\\{S_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) sunny lines and, by the argument of step\u202f2, still covers \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A covering by non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nA_c:\\; y=-x+c .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(A_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\); hence it is **not** sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) then \\(a+b=c\\) for the unique integer \\(c\\) equal to \\(a+b\\).  \nConsequently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=-a+(a+b)\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\in A_{a+b}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{A_c\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny lines that can replace non\u2011sunny ones  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_c:\\; y = x + (c-2) .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_c\\) is \\(+1\\); therefore \\(S_c\\) is sunny.  \nThe two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(1,c-1),\\qquad Q_c=(c-1,1)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nsatisfy the equations of both \\(A_c\\) and \\(S_c\\); indeed  \n\n\\[\nc-1 = -1 + c = 1 + (c-2).\n\\]\n\nNow take any point \\((a,b)\\in A_c\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(1\\le a,b\\le c-1\\).  \nIf \\(a=1\\) (resp. \\(b=1\\)) the point is \\(P_c\\) (resp. \\(Q_c\\)) and already lies on \\(S_c\\).  \nOtherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nu=a+1\\;( \\ge 3),\\qquad v=b+1\\;( \\ge 3).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(u+v = (a+b)+2 = c+2\\).  \nConsider the sunny lines \\(S_u\\) and \\(S_v\\):\n\\[\nS_u:\\; y = x + (u-2)=x+(a-1),\\qquad\nS_v:\\; y = x + (v-2)=x+(b-1).\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies both equations:\n\\[\nb = a + (a-1) \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b = 2a-1,\n\\]\nwhich holds because \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(c = a+b = a + (c-a)