            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** when its direction is *different* from the three\nforbidden directions: horizontal \\((\\text{slope }0)\\), vertical \\((\\text{slope }\\infty)\\) and the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) \\((\\text{slope }-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one line must be non‑sunny\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points\n\\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in T_n\\).\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) equals both \\(\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\) and\n\\(\\frac{b_3-b_2}{a_3-a_2}\\).  Since all coordinates are positive integers,\neach of these quotients is either \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); otherwise the\ndifference of the two quotients would be a non‑zero rational number with\ndenominator \\(1\\), forcing the slope to be one of the three forbidden\nvalues.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the\n\\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), contradicting the definition of sunny.\n∎\n\nConsequently, if all \\(n\\) lines were sunny they could cover at most \\(2n\\)\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  Because  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\ncovering all points of \\(T_n\\) with only sunny lines is impossible.  Hence at\nleast one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, and therefore\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) satisfying \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n**Lines used**\n\n1. **One non‑sunny line**  \n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **\\(n-1-k\\) further non‑sunny lines** (vertical)  \n   \\[\n   V_j:\\;x=j ,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k .\n   \\]\n\n3. **\\(k\\) sunny lines** (slope \\(1\\))  \n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y=x+i ,\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1 .\n   \\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct; the vertical lines are parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis, the diagonal \\(D\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), and the lines\n\\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so they are sunny.\n\n**Coverage of the points of \\(T_n\\).**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1.* \\(a\\le n-1-k\\).  \nThen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n*Case 2.* \\(a>n-1-k\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\le k+1\\).\n\n • If \\(b\\le k\\) then \\(b-a\\) is an integer between \\(- (n-1-k)\\) and\n\\(k-1\\).  Because \\(a>n-1-k\\), we have \\(b-a\\ge0\\); consequently\n\\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\) for some\n\\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n • If \\(b=k+1\\) then the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(a\\le n-k\\).  Since we are in the subcase \\(a>n-1-k\\), the only possible\nvalue is \\(a=n-k\\).  The point \\((n-k,k+1)\\) satisfies\n\\((n-k)+(k+1)=n+1\\neq2\\); therefore it is not on \\(D\\).  However,\n\\((n-k,k+1)\\) also satisfies \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) when \\(k\\ge1\\), which would\nplace it on a vertical line already counted in Case 1.  The only point of\n\\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=2\\) is \\((1,1)\\); this point is covered by the diagonal\n\\(D\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed\nlines.\n\n**Counting sunny lines.**  \nThe lines \\(S_i\\) (\\(k\\) of them) are sunny, while the line \\(D\\) and the\nvertical lines \\(V_j\\) are non‑sunny.  Hence the total number of sunny\nlines equals \\(k\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{the diagonal }D)+(n-1-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})=n .\n\\]\n\nTherefore for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and having\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nLemma 1 shows that at least one line must be non‑sunny, giving the necessary\ncondition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  The explicit construction above shows that every\ninteger \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can indeed be realized.  Consequently\nthe set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
