bidden diagonal, and the lines\n   \\(S_i\\) have slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), hence they are sunny.\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   - **Case\u202f1.** \\(a\\le n-1-k\\).  \n     Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (non\u2011sunny).\n\n   - **Case\u202f2.** \\(a>n-1-k\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1 .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(b\\le k+1\\).\n\n     *Subcase\u202f2a.* \\(b\\le k\\).  \n     Then \\(b-a\\) is an integer satisfying  \n\n     \\[\n     0\\le b-a\\le k-1 ,\n     \\]\n\n     because \\(a>n-1-k\\) forces \\(b-a\\ge0\\).  Consequently \\(b-a=i\\) for some\n     \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=x+i\\) and therefore\n     lies on the sunny line \\(S_i\\).\n\n     *Subcase\u202f2b.* \\(b=k+1\\).  \n     The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n-k\\).  Since we are in\n     Case\u202f2 we have \\(a>n-1-k\\), whence \\(a=n-k\\).  Thus the point is\n     \\((n-k,k+1)\\).  Observe that  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-k)+(k+1)=n+1\\neq2 ,\n     \\]\n\n     so it is **not** on the diagonal \\(D\\).  However, because \\(k\\le n-1\\) we\n     have \\(n-k\\ge1\\) and \\(k+1\\ge1\\); the point \\((n-k,k+1)\\) satisfies\n     \\(a\\le n-1-k\\) only when \\(k=0\\), which is excluded from Subcase\u202f2b.\n     Hence the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(b=k+1\\) and \\(a>n-1-k\\) is\n     \\((1,1)\\) (the case \\(k=n-1\\) gives \\(a=1,b=n\\), which is covered by\n     \\(V_1\\)).  The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\) because\n     \\(1+1=2\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is contained in at least one\n     of the constructed lines.\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{the diagonal }D)+(n-1-k)\\;(\\text{vertical})+k\\;(\\text{sunny})=n,\n   \\]\n\n   and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 yields the necessary condition \\