0,t)\\) or \\((t,-t)\\) for some integer \\(t\\neq0\\); these correspond\n   respectively to a line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, to the \\(y\\)-axis or to the\n   line \\(x+y=0\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is sunny, none of these three possibilities\n   can occur, a contradiction.  Hence at most two points of \\(T_n\\) lie on a\n   sunny line. ∎\n\n   Because \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), covering all points\n   of \\(T_n\\) with only sunny lines would require more than \\(n\\) lines.\n   Consequently at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny, giving the\n   necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-1. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sufficiency: construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   Fix such a \\(k\\).  We exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\n   sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Lines used.*\n\n   * one non‑sunny line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\):\n     \\[\n     D:\\;x+y=2;\n     \\]\n   * \\(n-1-k\\) vertical non‑sunny lines:\n     \\[\n     V_j:\\;x=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1-k);\n     \\]\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\):\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y=x+i\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n     \\]\n\n   All these lines are distinct.  The vertical lines are parallel to the\n   \\(y\\)-axis, \\(D\\) is parallel to the forbidden diagonal, and each \\(S_i\\)\n   has slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the \\(S_i\\) are precisely the sunny\n   lines.\n\n   *Verification that every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of the\n   constructed lines.*\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n   **Case 1.** \\(a\\le n-1-k\\).  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (non‑sunny).\n\n   **Case 2.** \\(a> n-1-k\\).  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k)=k+1. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(b\\le k+1\\).\n\n   *Subcase 2a.* \\(b\\le k\\).  \n   Set \\(i=b-a\\).  Because \