to the prohibited directions.  \n     For instance, for each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) that does **not** appear as the sum \\(a+b\\) of a point already used on a sunny line, we introduce the line \\(x+y=s\\). Because there are at most \\(n-k\\) such sums, we can assign a distinct line to each of them; the lines are all parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \n\n   All lines are distinct, the required points are covered, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny. Hence every integer \\(k\\) in the interval is attainable.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit constructions we obtain the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if it is not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad L_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad L_{-1}:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) on a sunny line\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line that meets \\(T_n\\).  \nWrite a direction vector of \\(\\ell\\) as \\((p,q)\\) where \\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z\\) are coprime.  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to \\(L_x\\), \\(L_y\\) or \\(L_{-1}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a_0,b_0)\\in T_n\\cap\\ell\\), then every lattice point of \\(\\ell\\) is of the form  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+tp,\\;b_0+tq),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(a_0+b_0)+t(p+q)\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince by (1) we have \\(p+q\\neq0\\), (3) can hold for at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-(a_0+b_0)}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ninteger values of \\(t\\).  \nThe smallest possible value of \\(|p+q|\\) under (1) is \\(2\\) (the only way to have \\(|p+q|=1\\) would be \\((p,q)=(1,0)\\) or \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\), all excluded).  \nThe smallest possible value of \\(a_0+b_0\\) is \\(2\\) (the point \\((1,1)\\)).  \nSubstituting these minima into (4) yields the maximal possible number of points of \\(T_n\\) that can lie on a sunny line:\n\n\\[\nM_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_n\\)**, and this bound is attained, for example, by the line \\(y=2x-1\\) (direction \\((1,2)\\)), which contains exactly the points \\((1,1),(2,3),\\dots\\) up to the limit imposed by \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\), and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nLet the sunny lines be \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).  \nBy (5) each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains at most \\(M_n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); hence the total number of points that can be covered by the sunny lines is at most \\(kM_n\\).\n\nAll remaining points of \\(T_n\\) must be covered by the other \\(n-k\\) lines.  \nSince a line parallel to one of the prohibited directions may contain arbitrarily many points of \\(T_n\\), there is **no** numerical restriction coming from those lines; the only restriction is that the sunny lines cannot be forced to cover more points than their capacity \\(M_n\\).\n\nConsequently we must have  \n\n\\[\nkM_n\\ge |T_n|-\\bigl(\\text{points that can be placed on the }n-k\\text{ non‑sunny lines}\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is at least \\(|T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\) (the worst case is that each non‑sunny line also contains at most \\(M_n\\) points).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nkM_n\\ge |T_n|-(n-k)M_n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le\\frac{|T_n|}{M_n}- (n-k)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence any feasible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence not sunny) and contains exactly the points of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates sum to \\(s\\). Their union is precisely \\(T_n\\). Thus \\(k=0\\) is realizable.\n\n#### 3.2  The case \\(1\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines of slope \\(2\\) (direction \\((1,2)\\)):\n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=2x-(2i-1)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the integer points of \\(\\ell_i\\) lying in \\(T_n\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,3),\\;(3,5),\\dots ,\\bigl(t_i,\\,2t_i-(2i-1)\\bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhere the largest admissible \\(t_i\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nt_i+2t_i-(2i-1)\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nt_i\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2i}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence the number of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2i}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor-(i-1).\n\\]\n\nA direct verification (using \\(k\\le\\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\)) shows that  \n\n\\[\nm_i\\ge M\\quad\\text{for each }i,\n\\]\nand that the total number of points covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\sum_{i=1}^{k} m_i = kM .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nNow remove from \\(T_n\\) the \\(S_k\\) points already placed on the sunny lines.  \nThe remaining set has size  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-S_k = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 - kM .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) that does **not** occur as the sum \\(a+b\\) of a point already used on a sunny line, introduce the line  \n\n\\[\nx+y=s .\n\\]\n\nBecause each such line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) it is non‑sunny.  \nThe number of distinct sums that appear among the points on the sunny lines is exactly \\(k\\) (one new sum for each \\(\\ell_i\\)), therefore the number of remaining sums is at most \\(n-k\\). Consequently we need at most \\(n-k\\) additional lines, and we have exactly \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines available.\n\nThus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFrom (6) we have the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe constructions of Sections 3.1 and 3.2 show that **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying this inequality can indeed be realized.  \n\nHence the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
