ing \\(T_n\\) the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \u201canti\u2011diagonals\u2019\u2019  \n\n\\[\nD_m=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=m\\},\\qquad m=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(D_m\\) contains exactly \\(m-1\\) points.  \nA line of slope \\(0\\) (horizontal) meets each \\(D_m\\) in at most one point, because all points of a fixed \\(D_m\\) have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates.  \nSimilarly a line of slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) meets each \\(D_m\\) in at most one point, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) meets each \\(D_m\\) in at most one point (all points of a given \\(D_m\\) have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates).  \n\nHence any **non\u2011sunny** line can intersect at most two distinct anti\u2011diagonals: a horizontal line can intersect at most two of the sets \\(D_m\\) (namely those with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate), and the same holds for vertical and slope \\(-1\\) lines.  \n\nSince there are \\(n\\) anti\u2011diagonals altogether, covering all of them with non\u2011sunny lines would require at least \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) such lines.  \nBut we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, and the problem asks for the *minimum* possible number of sunny lines, i.e. the *maximum* possible number of non\u2011sunny lines.  \nConsequently the number of non\u2011sunny lines is at most \\(2\\); otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines to hit every anti\u2011diagonal.  \n\nThus at least \\(n-2\\) lines must be sunny.  \nBecause a single sunny line can intersect **two** anti\u2011diagonals (its slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\)), we can improve the bound by observing that each sunny line can cover at most two anti\u2011diagonals that are not already covered by the two possible non\u2011sunny lines.  \nHence the number of sunny lines needed is at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f2 (upper bound)\n\n*Claim.* For every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nthere exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe first construct a configuration with **\\(n\\) sunny lines**.  \nChoose \\(n\\) distinct slopes \\(\\alpha_1,\\dots ,\\alpha_n\\) none of which belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  \nFor each \\(m=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) pick a point \\((a_m,b_m)\\in D_m\\) (for instance \\((1,m-1)\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-b_{i+1}= \\alpha_i (x-a_{i+1}),\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\]\n\nBecause the slopes are distinct, the lines are distinct.  \nEach line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((a_{i+1},b_{i+1})\\in D_{i+1}\\); therefore every anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_m\\) contains at least one of the lines, and consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line.  \nAll \\(L_i\\) are sunny, so we have a configuration with \\(k=n\\).\n\nTo reduce the number of sunny lines we replace some of the sunny lines by **non\u2011sunny** ones while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Step 1 (introducing two non\u2011sunny lines).*  \nTake the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH:\\; y=1\n\\]\n\nand the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV:\\; x=1 .\n\\]\n\nBoth are non\u2011sunny.  They together cover all points of the first anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and also each cover one point of every other anti\u2011diagonal: \\(H\\) meets \\(D_m\\) at \\((m-1,1)\\) and \\(V\\) meets \\(D_m\\) at \\((1,m-1)\\).  \n\nThus after adding \\(H\\) and \\(V\\) we may delete the two sunny lines that originally covered the points \\((m-1,1)\\) and \\((1,m-1)\\) for each \\(m\\).  The total number of lines remains \\(n\\), but now exactly two lines are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Step 2 (trading a sunny line for a slope \\(-1\\) line).*  \nIf we still need to reduce the number of sunny lines, we can introduce a line of slope \\(-1\\),\n\n\\[\nS:\\; x+y=2 .\n\\]\n\nIt passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,0)\\) (the latter is not in \\(T_n\\) but the former is).  \nBy suitably choosing which sunny line to delete (namely the one that was covering \\((1,1)\\)), we replace a sunny line by the non\u2011sunny line \\(S\\).  \n\nRepeating this operation we can replace up to \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines by non\u2011sunny ones, because each replacement removes two uncovered points from the set of anti\u2011diagonals and a non\u2011sunny line can handle at most two of them.  \n\nConsequently we can achieve any number of sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nk = n - t ,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, every integer  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nis attainable.  \n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1 gives the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\n\\]\n\nand Lemma\u202f2 shows that this condition is also sufficient.  \nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\big|\\;\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\,\\right\\}} .\n\\]"
